<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ephorus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400–330 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ephorus of Cyme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ephorus of Cyme was a Greek historian of the 4th century BCE, said to have been a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a>. His Histories in 30 books were the first universal history, arranged by subject from the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heracleidae</a> to 340 BCE. The work is lost but was used heavily by later writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>.</p></div>
